/*
 * ============================================================================
 * Zamani Universal Programming Language
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * FILE
 * ----
 * grammar/interoperability/interoperability.g4
 *
 * GRAMMAR
 * -------
 * Interoperability
 *
 * STATUS
 * ------
 * CANONICAL INTEROPERABILITY COMPOSITION GRAMMAR
 *
 * LANGUAGE
 * --------
 * Zamani
 *
 * ANTLR
 * ----
 * ANTLR4 parser grammar
 *
 * RUST IMPLEMENTATION BASELINE
 * -----------------------------
 * Rust 1.97+
 * Rust 2021
 * Safe Rust only
 * No unsafe Rust
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * FEATURE CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * PURPOSE
 * -------
 *
 * This file is the canonical composition boundary for source-level
 * interoperability in Zamani.
 *
 * It composes independently owned interoperability grammars into the stable
 * parser-facing interoperability boundary consumed by:
 *
 *     grammar/antlr/ZamaniParser.g4
 *
 * It answers:
 *
 *     "Which interoperability constructs participate in canonical
 *      Zamani source composition?"
 *
 * It does NOT answer:
 *
 *     "How is an interoperability construct implemented?"
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * OWNERSHIP
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * THIS FILE OWNS
 * --------------
 *
 * Only:
 *
 *     interoperabilityDeclaration
 *     interoperabilityStatement
 *     interoperabilityExpression
 *
 * plus intentionally narrow composition aliases used to expose independently
 * owned interoperability families to tooling and downstream grammar
 * composition.
 *
 * THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN
 * ----------------------
 *
 * This file does NOT own the syntax of:
 *
 *     names
 *     identifiers
 *     qualified names
 *     attributes
 *     expressions
 *     types
 *     parameters
 *     generic parameters
 *     visibility
 *     ABI
 *     FFI
 *     foreign functions
 *     foreign types
 *     calling conventions
 *     data layouts
 *     linkage
 *     serialization
 *     C
 *     C++
 *     Rust
 *     Python
 *     Zig
 *     OpenQASM
 *     QIR
 *     Verilog
 *     SystemVerilog
 *     assembly
 *     WebAssembly
 *     system interfaces
 *
 * Those constructs remain owned by their dedicated grammars.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * SINGLE-AUTHORITY RULE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Exactly one grammar must own each general syntax family.
 *
 * Ownership:
 *
 *     names
 *         -> grammar/core/names.g4
 *
 *     attributes
 *         -> grammar/core/attributes.g4
 *
 *     expressions
 *         -> grammar/expressions/expressions.g4
 *
 *     types
 *         -> grammar/types/types.g4
 *
 *     parameters
 *         -> grammar/functions/parameters.g4
 *
 *     function generics
 *         -> grammar/functions/generics.g4
 *
 *     ABI
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/abi.g4
 *
 *     FFI
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/ffi.g4
 *
 *     foreign identity/source metadata
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/foreign.g4
 *
 *     foreign callable declarations
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/foreign-functions.g4
 *
 *     foreign types
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/foreign-types.g4
 *
 *     calling conventions
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/calling-conventions.g4
 *
 *     data layout
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/data-layout.g4
 *
 *     linkage
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/linkage.g4
 *
 *     serialization interoperability façade
 *         -> grammar/interoperability/serialization.g4
 *
 * This file MUST NOT reproduce those rules.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * ARCHITECTURAL POSITION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 *                         ZAMANI SOURCE
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                         ZamaniLexer
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                         ZamaniParser
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                    Interoperability
 *                              |
 *        +---------------------+----------------------+
 *        |          |          |          |            |
 *        v          v          v          v            v
 *       ABI        FFI     Foreign     Foreign     Serialization
 *                         Functions     Types
 *        |          |          |          |            |
 *        +----------+----------+----------+------------+
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                     Domain-neutral AST
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                       Structural validation
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                       Semantic analysis
 *                              |
 *        +---------------------+----------------------+
 *        |                     |                      |
 *        v                     v                      v
 *     Classical            quantum::ir          HDL/Hardware
 *        |                     |                      |
 *        +---------------------+----------------------+
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                       Canonical semantic model
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                           Canonical IR
 *                              |
 *                    +---------+---------+
 *                    |         |         |
 *                    v         v         v
 *                 optimize   lower    specialize
 *                              |
 *                    +---------+---------+
 *                    |         |         |
 *                    v         v         v
 *                 routing  scheduling resilience
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                          ZQN / QEC
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                             HAL
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                       target realization
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * COMPOSITION DIRECTION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Composition MUST remain one-directional:
 *
 *     leaf grammar
 *          |
 *          v
 *     interoperability composition
 *          |
 *          v
 *     ZamaniParser
 *
 * Leaf grammars MUST NOT import ZamaniParser.
 *
 * This prevents circular grammar dependencies.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * LEXER AUTHORITY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The canonical parser-facing lexer is:
 *
 *     grammar/antlr/ZamaniLexer.g4
 *
 * Therefore:
 *
 *     tokenVocab = ZamaniLexer;
 *
 * is mandatory.
 *
 * This grammar MUST NOT define lexer rules.
 *
 * It MUST NOT introduce a second token vocabulary.
 *
 * It MUST NOT use:
 *
 *     tokenVocab = ZamaniTokens;
 *
 * as the parser-facing vocabulary.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * IMPORT MODEL
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * ANTLR imports use parser grammar identities rather than filesystem paths.
 *
 * The composition dependencies are:
 *
 *     Ffi
 *     Abi
 *     InteroperabilityForeign
 *     ForeignFunctions
 *     InteroperabilityForeignTypes
 *     InteroperabilityCallingConventions
 *     InteroperabilityDataLayout
 *     InteroperabilityLinkage
 *     SerializationInterop
 *
 * Their filesystem locations are:
 *
 *     grammar/interoperability/ffi.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/abi.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/foreign.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/foreign-functions.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/foreign-types.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/calling-conventions.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/data-layout.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/linkage.g4
 *     grammar/interoperability/serialization.g4
 *
 * The build system MUST make those parser grammar roots available to ANTLR.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * POCO-REAF CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability expresses portable semantic intent.
 *
 * It MUST NOT encode a particular physical realization.
 *
 * Therefore this grammar contains no universal language-level limits on:
 *
 *     CPU count
 *     core count
 *     thread count
 *     GPU count
 *     FPGA count
 *     ASIC count
 *     accelerator count
 *     QPU count
 *     qubit count
 *     node count
 *     device count
 *     memory capacity
 *     storage capacity
 *     network size
 *     tensor rank
 *     tensor dimensions
 *     register width
 *     address-space size
 *     interface count
 *     function count
 *     parameter count
 *     argument count
 *     callback count
 *     metadata count
 *     schema size
 *
 * The grammar MUST NOT introduce any artificial capacity constants such as:
 *
 *     MAX_QUBITS
 *     MAX_CPUS
 *     MAX_GPUS
 *     MAX_FPGAS
 *     MAX_NODES
 *     MAX_MEMORY
 *     MAX_THREADS
 *     MAX_TENSOR_RANK
 *     MAX_REGISTER_WIDTH
 *     MAX_NETWORK_SIZE
 *     MAX_DEVICE_COUNT
 *
 * Nor may it introduce interoperability-specific equivalents such as:
 *
 *     MAX_FOREIGN_FUNCTIONS
 *     MAX_FOREIGN_TYPES
 *     MAX_FFI_INTERFACES
 *     MAX_ABIS
 *     MAX_CALLBACKS
 *     MAX_METADATA
 *
 * Repetition uses ANTLR repetition operators and remains open-ended from the
 * language-definition perspective.
 *
 * Physical feasibility is determined downstream by:
 *
 *     resource analysis
 *     capability analysis
 *     policy
 *     compiler implementation resources
 *     runtime resources
 *     target resources
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * DETERMINISM CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Parsing MUST depend only on:
 *
 *     source text
 *     lexer configuration
 *     grammar version
 *     grammar composition
 *
 * Parsing MUST NOT depend on:
 *
 *     hardware availability
 *     filesystem state
 *     network state
 *     environment variables
 *     current time
 *     randomness
 *     linker state
 *     library availability
 *     target availability
 *     runtime state
 *
 * Identical source and identical grammar composition MUST produce equivalent
 * parse structure.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * INERTNESS / SECURITY CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This grammar performs NO:
 *
 *     library loading
 *     dynamic linking
 *     symbol resolution
 *     filesystem access
 *     network access
 *     process execution
 *     hardware discovery
 *     device discovery
 *     QPU access
 *     HDL-tool invocation
 *     linker invocation
 *     loader invocation
 *     resource allocation
 *     physical-address access
 *     runtime execution
 *
 * It is a declarative source grammar only.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * ROOT PARSER CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * grammar/antlr/ZamaniParser.g4 defines:
 *
 *     interoperabilityElement
 *         : interoperabilityDeclaration
 *         | interoperabilityStatement
 *         | interoperabilityExpression
 *         ;
 *
 * This file therefore MUST export exactly those three stable composition
 * boundaries.
 *
 * Do not replace them with one generic:
 *
 *     interoperability
 *
 * rule.
 *
 * The three-way split is important because declaration, statement, and
 * expression contexts have different AST and semantic ownership.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * FOREIGN LANGUAGE CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This file does NOT parse complete foreign languages.
 *
 * Foreign language grammars remain independent:
 *
 *     C
 *     C++
 *     Rust
 *     Python
 *     Zig
 *     OpenQASM
 *     QIR
 *     Verilog
 *     SystemVerilog
 *     assembly
 *     WebAssembly
 *     system-interface formats
 *
 * A foreign source can be:
 *
 *     1. referenced symbolically by Zamani;
 *     2. parsed by its own frontend;
 *     3. semantically converted into the canonical Zamani semantic model;
 *     4. lowered into the appropriate canonical IR.
 *
 * Complete foreign-language syntax MUST NOT be copied into this file.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * QUANTUM CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may connect Zamani with quantum software, providers,
 * simulators, services, or foreign quantum representations.
 *
 * This grammar MUST NOT define:
 *
 *     quantum gates
 *     physical qubits
 *     qubit topology
 *     routing
 *     scheduling
 *     calibration
 *     QEC implementation
 *     ZQN
 *     HAL
 *
 * Quantum meaning MUST eventually converge through:
 *
 *     semantic analysis
 *          |
 *          v
 *     quantum::ir
 *
 * No second quantum interoperability IR is permitted.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * HDL / HARDWARE CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may describe an HDL or hardware boundary, but this file
 * MUST NOT define:
 *
 *     physical pins
 *     fixed bus widths
 *     fixed register widths
 *     device IDs
 *     FPGA capacities
 *     ASIC capacities
 *     accelerator counts
 *     physical addresses
 *     fixed topologies
 *
 * Hardware and HDL semantics remain downstream.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * DECLARATION COMPOSITION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * `interoperabilityDeclaration` is the canonical declaration dispatcher.
 *
 * It delegates complete declaration forms to their owners.
 *
 * IMPORTANT:
 *
 * The order below is architectural, not a semantic preference.
 *
 * More specific declaration families are kept explicit so that future grammar
 * changes cannot accidentally turn this file into an implicit "first
 * alternative wins" semantic resolver.
 *
 * If two imported declaration families acquire overlapping token prefixes,
 * that overlap MUST be resolved in the owning grammars or by introducing an
 * explicit discriminator there.
 *
 * This file MUST NOT resolve ambiguity by semantic predicates or parser
 * actions.
 * ============================================================================
 */

parser grammar Interoperability;

options {
    tokenVocab = ZamaniLexer;
}

import
    Ffi,
    Abi,
    InteroperabilityForeign,
    ForeignFunctions,
    InteroperabilityForeignTypes,
    InteroperabilityCallingConventions,
    InteroperabilityDataLayout,
    InteroperabilityLinkage,
    SerializationInterop
;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 1. CANONICAL DECLARATION ENTRY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * ABI declarations:
 *
 *     Abi
 *
 * FFI declarations:
 *
 *     Ffi
 *
 * Foreign callable declarations:
 *
 *     ForeignFunctions
 *
 * Foreign type declarations:
 *
 *     InteroperabilityForeignTypes
 *
 * Serialization contracts:
 *
 *     SerializationInterop
 *
 * Calling-convention, data-layout, and linkage declarations are deliberately
 * NOT independently added here unless their owning grammars expose a complete
 * source declaration intended for top-level Zamani composition.
 *
 * Their normal role is as contracts referenced by ABI/FFI/foreign declarations.
 *
 * This distinction prevents the composition grammar from manufacturing
 * top-level declarations that have no corresponding language-level AST
 * ownership.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityDeclaration
    : abiDeclaration
    | ffiForeignDeclaration
    | interoperabilityForeignFunctionDeclaration
    | interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
    | serializationInteroperabilityContract
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 2. CANONICAL STATEMENT ENTRY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Statement-level interoperability operations are owned by Ffi or the
 * serialization façade.
 *
 * No foreign-call syntax is duplicated here.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityStatement
    : ffiCallStatement
    | ffiCallbackCallStatement
    | serializationInteroperabilityStatement
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 3. CANONICAL EXPRESSION ENTRY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Expression-level interoperability operations are delegated to the FFI and
 * serialization owners.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityExpression
    : ffiCallExpression
    | ffiCallbackCallExpression
    | ffiCallableReferenceExpression
    | serializationInteroperabilityExpression
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 4. FFI COMPOSITION ENTRY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This is a stable adapter for tools that need one complete FFI item.
 *
 * Ffi remains the owner of the actual syntax.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityFfiItem
    : ffiItem
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 5. ABI COMPOSITION ENTRIES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * These aliases expose ABI constructs without taking ownership of their
 * internals.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityAbiDeclaration
    : abiDeclaration
    ;


interoperabilityAbiContract
    : abiContract
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 6. FOREIGN-FUNCTION COMPOSITION ENTRY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * IMPORTANT:
 *
 * This is deliberately NOT:
 *
 *     interoperabilityForeignFunctionDeclaration
 *         : interoperabilityForeignFunctionDeclaration
 *         ;
 *
 * That form is infinite self-recursion and is invalid.
 *
 * The actual owner is:
 *
 *     ForeignFunctions
 *
 * Therefore this adapter delegates to the public rule exported by that
 * grammar.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityForeignFunctionDeclaration
    : foreignFunctionDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 7. FOREIGN-TYPE COMPOSITION ENTRIES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * ForeignTypes owns the syntax.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
    : interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 8. SERIALIZATION COMPOSITION ENTRIES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * SerializationInterop is already a façade over the canonical data
 * serialization grammar.
 *
 * These adapters do not duplicate serialization syntax.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilitySerializationUnit
    : serializationInteroperabilityUnit
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationConstruct
    : serializationInteroperabilityConstruct
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationStatement
    : serializationInteroperabilityStatement
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationExpression
    : serializationInteroperabilityExpression
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationContract
    : serializationInteroperabilityContract
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationSerialize
    : serializationInteroperabilitySerialize
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationDeserialize
    : serializationInteroperabilityDeserialize
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationSerializeExpression
    : serializationInteroperabilitySerializeExpression
    ;


interoperabilitySerializationDeserializeExpression
    : serializationInteroperabilityDeserializeExpression
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 9. FOREIGN IDENTITY COMPOSITION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Foreign identity remains owned by:
 *
 *     grammar/interoperability/foreign.g4
 *
 * This adapter exists only to expose the symbolic boundary to interoperability
 * tooling.
 *
 * The identity is symbolic.
 *
 * It does not:
 *
 *     resolve a library;
 *     resolve a process;
 *     load a runtime;
 *     select a device;
 *     select a CPU;
 *     select a GPU;
 *     select a QPU;
 *     select a network endpoint.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityForeignIdentity
    : foreignIdentity
    ;


interoperabilityForeignSource
    : foreignSource
    ;


interoperabilityForeignSourceReference
    : foreignSourceReference
    ;


interoperabilityForeignReference
    : foreignReference
    ;


interoperabilityForeignTargetReference
    : foreignTargetReference
    ;


interoperabilityForeignImplementationReference
    : foreignImplementationReference
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 10. ABI REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * ABI identity and ABI contracts remain owned by Abi.
 *
 * The symbolic alternatives do not force a closed-world list of ABIs.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityAbiReference
    : abiContract
    | qualifiedName
    | STRING_LITERAL
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 11. CALLING-CONVENTION REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Calling conventions remain symbolic and open-world.
 *
 * The composition layer does not enumerate conventional names.
 *
 * Examples that may be represented semantically include:
 *
 *     vendor::calling_convention
 *     platform::calling_convention
 *     runtime::calling_convention
 *
 * New conventions therefore do not require modifying this grammar.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityCallingConventionReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING_LITERAL
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 12. DATA-LAYOUT REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Data layout is a downstream interoperability/ABI concern.
 *
 * This adapter does not encode:
 *
 *     pointer width
 *     register width
 *     alignment limits
 *     address-space size
 *     word size
 *     object format
 *
 * Those properties are semantic target information.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityDataLayoutReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING_LITERAL
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 13. LINKAGE REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Linkage remains symbolic and open-world.
 *
 * This grammar does not enumerate static/dynamic/vendor-specific linkage
 * models as a finite language universe.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityLinkageReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING_LITERAL
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 14. CAPABILITY REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This is a symbolic reference only.
 *
 * It does NOT grant a capability.
 *
 * Authorization and capability realization belong to semantic analysis,
 * security, resources, policy, and target realization.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityCapabilityReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING_LITERAL
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 15. RESOURCE REFERENCE
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * A resource reference is symbolic.
 *
 * It does not allocate or reserve resources.
 *
 * Quantities and constraints belong to the canonical resource grammar and
 * semantic model.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityResourceReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING_LITERAL
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 16. METADATA
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * General metadata is represented through canonical attributes.
 *
 * This composition layer does not invent an interoperability-specific
 * metadata language.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityMetadata
    : attribute
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * 17. COMPOSITION CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The following semantic boundaries MUST remain separate downstream:
 *
 *     foreign identity
 *          !=
 *     ABI
 *          !=
 *     calling convention
 *          !=
 *     data layout
 *          !=
 *     linkage
 *          !=
 *     FFI
 *          !=
 *     foreign type
 *          !=
 *     foreign function
 *          !=
 *     serialization
 *
 * A complete interoperability semantic object may reference several of these
 * simultaneously, but none may silently become another.
 *
 * Example:
 *
 *     Foreign function
 *         |
 *         +--> foreign identity
 *         +--> foreign type
 *         +--> ABI
 *         +--> calling convention
 *         +--> linkage
 *         +--> data layout
 *         +--> effects
 *         +--> capabilities
 *         +--> resources
 *         +--> contracts
 *         +--> policies
 *         +--> provenance
 *
 * Semantic analysis combines these contracts.
 *
 * The grammar merely preserves their structural boundaries.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 18. EFFECT INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability can imply effects such as:
 *
 *     foreign execution
 *     native interaction
 *     IO
 *     network
 *     mutation
 *     nondeterminism
 *     distributed communication
 *     quantum interaction
 *     hardware interaction
 *
 * This file MUST NOT define effect semantics.
 *
 * The effect system remains authoritative elsewhere.
 *
 * The semantic pipeline is:
 *
 *     interoperability syntax
 *          |
 *          v
 *     semantic interoperability contract
 *          |
 *          v
 *     effect analysis
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 19. CAPABILITY INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may require capabilities such as:
 *
 *     foreign execution
 *     serialization
 *     networking
 *     quantum execution
 *     hardware access
 *     accelerator access
 *     distributed execution
 *
 * Capability requirements remain declarative.
 *
 * They do not become target-selection commands.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 20. RESOURCE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may carry requirements on:
 *
 *     memory
 *     bandwidth
 *     latency
 *     throughput
 *     storage
 *     compute
 *     accelerator availability
 *     communication
 *     other resource dimensions
 *
 * Those requirements remain symbolic/semantic.
 *
 * This grammar introduces no resource ceilings.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 21. POLICY / SECURITY INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Foreign metadata MUST NOT grant permission.
 *
 * A source-level declaration that refers to:
 *
 *     capability
 *     provider
 *     trust
 *     permission
 *     policy
 *
 * is descriptive/contractual syntax.
 *
 * Authorization is determined downstream.
 *
 * Security analysis must be able to reject an otherwise syntactically valid
 * interoperability construct.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 22. PROVENANCE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability declarations may contribute provenance information such as:
 *
 *     foreign source identity
 *     provider identity
 *     implementation identity
 *     version
 *     transformation
 *     origin
 *
 * Provenance remains a semantic concern.
 *
 * The parser preserves source structure and source locations.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 23. COMPATIBILITY INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Compatibility is not determined by the parser.
 *
 * Semantic analysis may compare:
 *
 *     language versions
 *     ABI versions
 *     interface versions
 *     foreign type representations
 *     calling conventions
 *     linkage models
 *     serialization formats
 *     schema versions
 *     target capabilities
 *
 * A syntactically valid program may therefore still be semantically rejected
 * when the requested interoperability contract cannot be satisfied.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 24. QUANTUM INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * If interoperability crosses a quantum boundary:
 *
 *     foreign declaration
 *          |
 *          v
 *     semantic quantum operation/value/interface
 *          |
 *          v
 *     quantum::ir
 *
 * There MUST NOT be:
 *
 *     ForeignQuantumIR
 *     InteroperabilityQuantumIR
 *     ProviderSpecificQuantumIR
 *
 * introduced by this grammar.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 25. HDL / HARDWARE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * If interoperability crosses an HDL/hardware boundary:
 *
 *     interoperability
 *          |
 *          v
 *     hardware/HDL semantic model
 *          |
 *          v
 *     target/resource/capability analysis
 *
 * This grammar does not encode:
 *
 *     physical pins
 *     fixed bus widths
 *     fixed register widths
 *     device numbers
 *     board numbers
 *     FPGA capacities
 *     ASIC capacities
 *     accelerator counts
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 26. DISTRIBUTED / NETWORKING INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may represent distributed or network boundaries.
 *
 * It MUST NOT hard-code:
 *
 *     node 0
 *     CPU 0
 *     GPU 0
 *     device 0
 *     endpoint 0
 *
 * Physical placement and network realization remain downstream.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 27. SERIALIZATION INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Serialization is a representation boundary.
 *
 * The canonical syntax remains:
 *
 *     grammar/data/serialization.g4
 *
 * and its interoperability façade:
 *
 *     grammar/interoperability/serialization.g4
 *
 * This composition grammar does not redefine:
 *
 *     serialization
 *     deserialization
 *     serialization contracts
 *     serialization formats
 *     schemas
 *     codecs
 *     framing
 *     compression
 *     encoding
 *     integrity
 *     compatibility
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * 28. FOREIGN-LANGUAGE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Language-specific grammars remain separate.
 *
 * This composition grammar may provide symbolic identity and boundary
 * references, but it must never become a complete C/C++/Python/Rust/Zig/
 * OpenQASM/HDL parser.
 *
 * A new foreign language should normally be introducible by:
 *
 *     new leaf/dialect grammar
 *          +
